commit 606bdaf29ac525ecfdeca4c28bef8777432ae9e1
Author: Olof Johansson <olof@lixom.net>
Date:   Sun Sep 27 21:29:46 2026 -0700

    browser: keep the page's unresolved dark shell from painting

    KiroCrew 0.7.1 ships <html data-theme="dark">, keys all its colours to
    that attribute and writes the chosen theme onto <html> only after its
    module graph has booted. On a light phone every cold launch showed the
    dark shell for about 250 ms before the light dashboard (issue #4).

    Add a document-start <style> (PageScriptSources.calmShell) that, while
    <html> is still the static data-theme="dark" with no data-mode, offers
    color-scheme light dark, makes the body transparent and hides #root.
    The web view's own appearance-keyed backing shows until the page writes
    its choice, and the chosen theme paints the moment it does; nothing
    waits. The script removes the rule once data-mode appears, or 2 s after
    the app mounted without one. -UITestNoCalmShell leaves it out.

    Log every canvas colour the page reports with its time since the
    navigation began, so a device run says what painted first. The
    reporter now samples every frame for a document's first 3 s, listens
    for stylesheet loads, and reports a fading background as the colour it
    is heading for: the shell's dark default fades in and is turned round
    before it ends, and waiting for the fade hid it entirely.

    CalmLaunchTests, a new session-suite class, asserts the page never
    reports a canvas on the other side of the one it settles on, with a
    control under -UITestNoCalmShell; the list is shown by
    PageBackgroundInstrument under -UITestReportPageBackgrounds.
    scripts/measure-launch-flash.sh records the simulator through either
    and reports dark runs by per-frame luma.

    Spec: docs/features/F21-calm-cold-launch.md

